            <ArticleTitle>Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy presenting as acute subendocardial myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>329-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two patients with apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy presented with angina at rest and giant inverted T waves in precordial leads on the electrocardiogram. At cardiac catheterisation one patient had mild coronary artery disease, the other had normal coronary arteries. In both, there was a systolic pressure gradient between the apex and main left ventricular cavity exceeding 100 mmHg. The presence of rest angina with the electrocardiographic findings lead to a mistaken, initial diagnosis of acute subendocardial myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
